# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test environment - COMMITTED TO THE REPOSITORY, loaded by `npm test` via
# `tsx --env-file=.env.test`. See docs/architecture.md section 3.2.
#
# NEVER put credentials, tokens, keys, passwords or connection strings here.
# Only the non-secret keys allow-listed in tests/env-test-config.test.ts are
# permitted; that test fails the suite if anything else is added. Secrets
# for local work belong in `.env`, which stays gitignored.
#
# Every variable env.ts reads is pinned explicitly, to the same value as its
# built-in default. Why: env.ts also loads a developer's local `.env` through
# dotenv, and dotenv never overrides a variable that is already set - so
# pinning here makes the suite hermetic. A local `.env` with, say, a low
# RATE_LIMIT_MAX can no longer change test results.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# JWT configuration is deliberately NOT here. `JWT_SECRET` matches the
# SECRET_KEY_PATTERN that tests/env-test-config.test.ts forbids in this file
# by name, so the three JWT variables are pinned in
# tests/helpers/test-jwt-env.mjs instead, which `npm test` loads with
# `--import` before anything reads the environment. That file explains why.

# Drops the logger to errors only and keeps fakes constructible.
NODE_ENV=test

PORT=4000

# Empty on purpose: outside production an empty list means "use the dev
# localhost defaults" (src/config/cors.ts), which the security tests expect.
CORS_ORIGINS=

BODY_LIMIT=1mb
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=300
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MINUTES=15
